          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"ibsen-et-dukkehjem-22aa549e55c520e4\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Nora directly avows the cessation of her loving state: “Jeg elsker deg ikke mer” (“I no longer love you”). This is overwhelmingly truth-conditional. In the confrontation it also has weak force as a withdrawal from the marital relationship, but the sentence itself does not clearly undertake or promise a course of action. Its painful delivery does not make it an expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is slight T/P ambiguity because the avowal helps terminate the relationship, but its core wording describes Nora’s emotional state rather than explicitly enacting a commitment or separation.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Nora directly addresses Torvald and tells him that she no longer loves him.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Nora presents the disclosure as painful but unavoidable. Her acknowledgment that Torvald has been kind to her mitigates the interpersonal blow, while “Men jeg kan ikke gjøre ved det” (“But I cannot help it”) frames the loss of love as a state beyond voluntary control. This supports a considered, reluctant avowal rather than a reflexive emotional outburst.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jeg elsker deg ikke mer” (“I no longer love you”).\",\"supports\":\"Strong truth-conditional force: Nora explicitly presents the absence or cessation of love as true.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Å, det gjør meg så ondt, Torvald” (“Oh, it pains me so, Torvald”).\",\"supports\":\"The admission causes Nora pain, but this is contextual affect rather than independent evidence of expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Men jeg kan ikke gjøre ved det” (“But I cannot help it”).\",\"supports\":\"Frames the ceased love as an involuntary emotional condition and supports reading the utterance as an avowal of state.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The form is identified as negative, present-tense, cessative, with the temporal modifier “no longer.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the core proposition concerns the cessation of a previously existing loving state.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the direct avowal of a ceased emotional state; weak P captures limited relational-withdrawal pressure. No independent E force or residual O dimension is needed.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The negative cessative equivalent is classified by its utterance force, not as a positive declaration of love.\"}"
        }
      ],
